{"subjectId":"sql","id":"window-frames","title":"Running Totals & Window Frames","group":"Subqueries, CTEs & Windows","order":39,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The <strong>frame</strong> decides which rows around the current row a window aggregate sees: everything so far (running total), the last 3 rows (moving average), or the whole partition.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Frame syntax</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>{ROWS | RANGE | GROUPS} BETWEEN &lt;start&gt; AND &lt;end&gt;\n\n-- &lt;start&gt;/&lt;end&gt;:  UNBOUNDED PRECEDING | n PRECEDING | CURRENT ROW | n FOLLOWING | UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><code>OVER (…)</code></th><th>Default frame</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No <code>ORDER BY</code></td><td>The whole partition</td></tr><tr><td>With <code>ORDER BY</code></td><td><code>RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> — start to current row <em>including peers (ties)</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-arrows\"></i>Running total, moving average, centered window</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT day, amount,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS running_total,\n       ROUND(AVG(amount) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW), 1) AS moving_avg_3,\n       MAX(amount) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 1 PRECEDING AND 1 FOLLOWING) AS max_neighbourhood\nFROM daily_sales\nORDER BY day;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>day        | amount | running_total | moving_avg_3 | max_neighbourhood\n-----------+--------+---------------+--------------+------------------\n2024-03-01 | 120    | 120           | 120.0        | 120\n2024-03-02 | 80     | 200           | 100.0        | 150\n2024-03-03 | 150    | 350           | 116.7        | 150\n2024-03-04 | 90     | 440           | 106.7        | 200\n2024-03-05 | 200    | 640           | 146.7        | 200\n2024-03-06 | 60     | 700           | 116.7        | 200</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The first two moving averages use fewer than 3 rows (there is nothing before day 1). Filter them out or show them as incomplete if that matters.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>ROWS vs RANGE: the ties trap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, salary,\n       SUM(salary) OVER (ORDER BY salary)                                         AS range_default,\n       SUM(salary) OVER (ORDER BY salary ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS rows_frame\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 1\nORDER BY salary, emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary | range_default | rows_frame\n------+--------+---------------+-----------\nMeera | 95000  | 190000        | 95000\nKaran | 95000  | 190000        | 190000\nRavi  | 120000 | 310000        | 310000\nAsha  | 150000 | 460000        | 460000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Meera and Karan both earn 95000, so with the default <code>RANGE</code> frame they are <em>peers</em> and both see 190000. <code>ROWS</code> counts physical rows: 95000 then 190000. For running totals, specify <code>ROWS</code> (and a unique tie-breaker in <code>ORDER BY</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-layout-list\"></i>Running total per group, and a reset</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT customer_id, order_id, order_date,\n       COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date\n                      ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) AS nth_order_of_customer\nFROM orders\nORDER BY customer_id, order_date;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | order_id | order_date | nth_order_of_customer\n------------+----------+------------+----------------------\n1           | 101      | 2024-01-05 | 1\n1           | 103      | 2024-02-03 | 2\n1           | 108      | 2024-03-15 | 3\n2           | 102      | 2024-01-12 | 1\n2           | 106      | 2024-03-01 | 2\n3           | 104      | 2024-02-14 | 1\n3           | 107      | 2024-03-08 | 2\n4           | 105      | 2024-02-20 | 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-pie-2\"></i>Cumulative share (Pareto)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH product_rev AS (\n    SELECT p.name, SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS revenue\n    FROM order_items oi JOIN products p ON p.product_id = oi.product_id\n    GROUP BY p.product_id, p.name\n)\nSELECT name, revenue,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY revenue DESC ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING)\n             / SUM(revenue) OVER (), 1) AS cumulative_pct\nFROM product_rev\nORDER BY revenue DESC;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name       | revenue | cumulative_pct\n-----------+---------+---------------\nLaptop     | 107000  | 72.2\nDesk       | 16000   | 82.9\nChair      | 13500   | 92.0\nHeadphones | 10000   | 98.8\nNotebook   | 1500    | 99.8\nPen        | 300     | 100.0</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you calculate a running total in SQL?</summary><div><p><code>SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)</code>, adding <code>PARTITION BY</code> to restart per group. Without window functions: a correlated subquery <code>(SELECT SUM(amount) FROM t t2 WHERE t2.day &lt;= t.day)</code> — O(n²).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between ROWS and RANGE?</summary><div><p><code>ROWS</code> defines the frame by physical row count (2 PRECEDING = the two rows before). <code>RANGE</code> defines it by value of the <code>ORDER BY</code> key, so all rows with the same key (peers) are included together — which is why the default frame gives tied rows the same running total.</p></div></details></section>"}